Same template (built by the reference's code in prepare, pieces in the same order, variables in the same map shape) and the same scanner: look for two opening braces, find the first two closing braces, trim the name, look it up, copy the value or nothing, otherwise copy one byte. The reference's Strings are byte arrays here (8 MB text and rendered output, written a byte at a time) and its HashMap<String, String> is lib/strmap.mito mapping names to a number that selects one of 23 value texts, so a hit costs an extra table read and the copy is from that table; a name is cut out of the text with text_from, which copies its bytes into the request's text area where the reference borrows a slice. The text-area copy is dropped every loop iteration. Other stores are range-checked; state is unlogged, so nothing is saved for a rollback. Scratch state rebuilt every run (the reference's locals: rendered) is not logged (state is unlogged by default, so no undo log, as a Rust local has none).
